text:
the book environment, installed with pixi, in a folder called ~/bioinf-computing;
VS Code, the book’s editor, with its built-in terminal;
a first login to a cluster with ssh, and VS Code running there by your site’s supported route (if you have an account);
enough nano and vim to edit a file on a bare server.

the two FASTQ files from Chapter 1, moved in, checked, and protected from accidental changes;
a reference genome kept in a shared folder and linked in, not copied;
small tables that say which files belong to which sample;
a numbered script that counts the reads and writes a log of every run;
a README, a data record, and a notebook that say where everything came from.

ask a tool how to use it (--help, man, its website);
read an error message, and tell an error from a warning;
work through a short checklist that solves most problems on its own;
read a paper’s methods section for tools, versions, and parameters;
search well, and write a help request someone can answer;
check a command an AI assistant suggests before you trust it.





Terminal (laptop)

cd ~/bioinf-computing
